   181	`cudaLaunchKernelExC`, `cuLaunchKernel`, `cuLaunchKernelEx`; `cudaGraphLaunch`
   182	/ `cuGraphLaunch`). This nsys version's GPU trace carries no graph-id column
   183	even under `--cuda-graph-trace=node`, so GPU events are attributed by
   184	CorrId correlation: a kernel whose CorrId belongs to a graph-launch API call
   185	is captured; to a kernel-launch call, eager; neither → UNRESOLVED (counted,
   186	never assumed captured). API calls are windowed by the CPU-side NVTX pass
   187	range, kernels by the GPU-projected range. Fourfold classification per pass: (i) captured
   188	model computation (kernels inside replay); (ii) uncaptured model computation;
   189	(iii) external input staging (cudagraph-trees copies of non-static inputs
   190	into the static pool — `_foreach_copy_`/copy kernels/`cudaMemcpyAsync`);
   191	(iv) other uncaptured copies/memsets. Also reported: recorded-graph count
   192	(Inductor counters), graphs visited per pass, replay launches per pass, and
   193	compile/recording counter stability across settle → validation → timing.
   194	An arm is **fully graphed** only with positive replay evidence (≥1 graph
   195	launch in EVERY captured pass) AND zero uncaptured model kernels AND zero
   196	unresolved kernels in every pass (medians hide a defective minority); the
   197	captured pass count must equal the requested 50. Multiple graphs per pass
   198	are legitimate; staging (`_foreach_copy_`/multi_tensor_apply, direct-copy
   199	kernels, memcpy) is timed overhead but not uncaptured model work; bare
   200	elementwise kernels are model work.
   201	Arms verified: C1, C3, H (Inductor) and B, E, G (manual), from their
   202	session-2 traces at both operating points. No expectation is registered.
   203	
   204	## 4. Boundary-cost microbenchmark (plan 0.4; design registered, owner reviews)
   205	`fullmodel/boundary_bench.py`: chains of N **dependent** kernels, N ∈ {10,
   206	100, 1000, 2000}: (trivial) one Triton program loads ONE int32 element, adds
   207	1, stores it back to the same buffer — kernel k+1 depends on kernel k, and
   208	the final counter must equal the total launch count (exact-integer
   209	dependency proof); (elem37) an in-place value-preserving fp16 elementwise op
   210	on a 37 MB tensor, N ∈ {10, 100}; (gemm) alternating dependent GEMMs
   211	660×384×1536 / 660×1536×384 fp16 into preallocated ping-pong outputs with
   212	1/√K-scaled weights (bounded values), N ∈ {10, 100}. Modes: stream launches;
   213	one CUDA graph of the chain (replayed); programmatic dependent launch for the
   214	trivial chain (Triton 3.4 `launch_pdl=True`; `gdc_wait()` before the
   215	dependent load, `gdc_launch_dependents()` after the store; the PTX is checked
   216	for `griddepcontrol`), on a stream and inside a graph; real-kernel PDL is
   217	UNSUPPORTED through cuBLAS/ATen launches and recorded as such. Note: the
   218	triton 3.4.0 wheel's `language/extra/cuda/gdc.py` carries the pre-3.4
   219	`_builder=` signature and fails to compile under the 3.4 code generator
   220	(`_semantic=`); the bench therefore defines the same two one-line PTX
   221	intrinsics with the 3.4 signature (identical asm strings) — recorded here
   222	so the deviation from the library call is explicit. The elem37 chain's
   223	working set (37 MB) fits the RTX 5090's 96 MiB L2, so that chain measures
   224	the L2-resident rate, which is reported as the plan-1.4 L2 bandwidth input. Timing:
   225	unprofiled device time via CUDA events around the chain, median of 20
   226	repeats with min/max → `T(N)` per (chain, mode), plus least-squares slope
   227	and intercept over N. **`T(N)/N` and the slope are effective per-node service
   228	times INCLUDING kernel execution — not boundary costs.** A separate
   229	node-level trace of the same script yields, per chain segment (NVTX), the
   230	median kernel duration and the median serial inter-kernel gap; for non-PDL
   231	modes the serial gap is the boundary estimate; for PDL modes kernels overlap
   232	and no subtraction is attempted (pure boundary `UNRESOLVED`); the traced
   233	run's perturbation vs the unprofiled `T(N)` is reported. Prior calibration
   234	points to compare against: Hazy (H100 ≈ 2.1 µs stream, ≈ 1.3 µs graph);
   235	NVIDIA's CUDA 12.6 figure ≈ 0.55 µs/node is empty-kernel graph device
   236	service time (RTX 3060), not CPU launch cost. **Until measured, no
   237	per-boundary number is used anywhere**, and the ledger's X10 must not count
   238	kernel execution as removable boundary overhead. Trace-derived gaps are
   239	accepted only for cases whose dependency proof and PTX qualification passed
   240	(joined from the traced run's own JSON) and whose NVTX segment holds exactly
   241	N × 20 kernels; the perturbation runs (plan 1.6) are accepted only when all
   242	three runs are VALID, validation PASS, one block × 50 passes, manifest-bound
   243	with the same effective variant — otherwise the ratio AND the caveat are
   244	UNRESOLVED. Any unavailable mode is recorded `UNTESTED(reason)`.
   245	
   246	## 5. Session structure, protocol, budget
   247	- **Protocol 5a**, reused from `arms/common.py::timed_run` (per-iteration
   248	  synchronize latency block of 50 passes inside the cudaProfilerApi range;
   249	  one free-running block of 50 passes outside it), **five repeats per
   250	  process**; per-process statistic = min-of-medians and median spread; per
   251	  (arm × chunk) headline = **median over 3 fresh-process restarts of the
   252	  per-process min-of-medians**, every per-iteration list preserved
   253	  (an explicit extension of `sm120` README deviation 5; spread = max − min of
   254	  the repeat medians; within-process and between-process variation are both
   255	  preserved). **Plan 1.2 matched comparisons** use `fullmodel/matched_run.py`:
   256	  two arms built, settled and validated in ONE fresh process, then their 5a
   257	  blocks ALTERNATE (a, b, a, b, …, five each) so every block pair is matched
   258	  in time and clocks; repeated in three fresh processes. Registered pairs at
   259	  352800: (C1, C3) the graph-break tax; (C1, C1D) the DC-filter repair
   260	  alone; (C3, H) the pad-and-batch delta; (C1, C1A) once, to record the
   261	  expected compile failure. The matrix restarts are additionally round-major
   262	  (r1 of every arm, then r2, then r3). Clocks logged in the background at
   263	  1 Hz with timestamp and GPU UUID (`nvidia-smi --query-gpu=timestamp,uuid,
   264	  clocks.sm,clocks.mem,power.draw,temperature.gpu,utilization.gpu -l 1`) for
   265	  the whole session; timed blocks carry wall timestamps.
   266	  **Matched-pair acceptance:** both builds OK, both graph gates PASS, no
   267	  capped settle, a JOINT settle of the actual alternation (counters stable
   268	  across two full alternation rounds), both validations PASS after the joint
   269	  settle, counters stable across every block, and — joined at collection —
   270	  SDR PASS for both arms' cells at that operating point; otherwise the pair
   271	  is ineligible with reasons.
   272	  **Compile/recording stability:** a compiled run is eligible only if, across
   273	  settle → validation → timing → SDR, there are no new graph breaks or
   274	  unimplemented ops, the unique-graph count is unchanged, and every Inductor
   275	  cudagraph counter (recordings, skips, non-static-input recordings) is
   276	  unchanged; all snapshots are preserved in the run JSON.
   277	  **Provenance gate:** every process verifies the msst clone (HEAD = pin,
   278	  clean), the checkpoint sha256, the registered config path and the
   279	  explicit `use_amp` before building; the session preflight writes ONE
   280	  immutable `session_manifest.json` (repo commit + clean tree, sha256 of
   281	  this registration, of every measuring file, the clip manifest, pins,
   282	  environment) that every run and every reference is bound to; a mismatch
   283	  refuses the run.
   284	  **Headline rule:** a cell publishes a headline only if restarts r1, r2 AND
   285	  r3 all exist and are eligible (EXACTLY the 5×50 protocol, validation PASS,
   286	  recording state stable, manifest-bound, not a planted/traced/attribution
   287	  run), share ONE session manifest and ONE effective variant (capture scope
   288	  / adapter / compile mode / stream count), and the cell's SDR qualification
   289	  — taken only from the r1 record, same manifest and variant — is PASS; a
   290	  failed required restart is never replaced by a later tag. A matched pair
   291	  publishes a pair headline only if m1, m2 AND m3 are pair-eligible (5×50,
   292	  three matched restarts) under one manifest with one variant pair and both
   293	  arms' cells hold that SDR qualification; the matched runner re-checks each
   294	  arm's recording state after the joint settle and after validation.
   295	  **Recording observer:** compiled runs snapshot the cudagraph-trees
   296	  manager's recorded graph nodes (roots and descendants) together with the
   297	  dynamo/inductor counters at every phase boundary; a missing observer
   298	  leaves stability UNRESOLVED and the run ineligible.
   299	  **Runtime pins and manifest scope:** every process checks torch ==
   300	  2.8.0+cu128 and triton == 3.4.0 before building; the manifest binds the
   301	  complete measuring set (fullmodel/**, arms/*, scripts/*, configs/*,
   302	  frontend.py, frontend_rms_fused.py, setup.sh, this registration, the
   303	  runtime identity) and is compared field by field on every load; every
   304	  reference artifact (validation outputs, eager-A clip outputs) carries its
   305	  own lineage sidecar (manifest id + sha256) checked before use.
   306	- **Unprofiled timing is the primary result.** Session-2 traces are for
   307	  mechanism; profiled and unprofiled numbers never share a ratio. Profiling
   308	  perturbation (plan 1.6): A, C3, G at 4410 and 352800, wall unprofiled vs
   309	  `--cuda-graph-trace=graph` vs `=node`; >5 % inflation → every mechanism
   310	  number from that trace carries the factor as a caveat.
   311	- **Session 1 (≤ 3 h, RunPod secure-cloud RTX 5090, pod name
   312	  `melband_profiling`, image `runpod/pytorch:1.1.0-rc.154-cu1281-torch280-ubuntu2204`
   313	  = the pinned torch 2.8.0+cu128 / triton 3.4.0 matrix):** in this order —
   314	  setup (idempotent `setup.sh` + clip pack + nsys check); eager-A references
   315	  at both operating points; the two planted-fault checks; C3 operator
   316	  qualification per frame count; the matched pairs (§5, plan 1.2); the arm
   317	  matrix at 352800 and 4410 (three round-major restarts, SDR gate in r1 at
   318	  both points); C2 last. Budget guards (registered): a hard session deadline
   319	  (3 h) with a 15-min extraction reserve — a cell that cannot start before
   320	  the reserve is `UNTESTED(deadline)` and the session is INCOMPLETE, never
   321	  salvaged by weakening validation or timing; per-process caps (25 min
   322	  compile arms, 15 min others); an **aggregate C2 allowance of 40 min**, and
   323	  a timed-out cold C2 attempt is not retried at that operating point (the
   324	  remaining restarts are `UNTESTED`; a timeout means unavailable
   325	  performance, not evidence that C2 is slow). Every run's JSON carries
   326	  `headline_eligible` with machine-readable reasons; a headline per (arm ×
   327	  operating point) requires three complete eligible restarts with the same
   328	  effective variant (capture scope / adapter / compile mode), else the cell
   329	  is `INCOMPLETE`. At the end of each session `fullmodel/completeness.py`
   330	  audits the registered mandatory items and writes the mechanical session
   331	  verdict (COMPLETE / INCOMPLETE with per-item reasons) to
   332	  `session_status.json`; supervisor messages go to stderr and every bounded
   333	  auxiliary step leaves a terminal `aux_<name>.json` (OK / FAILED /
   334	  UNTESTED). Pod setup (image boot, `setup.sh`) precedes the session clock.
   335	  Evidence extraction before terminate.
   336	- **Session 2 (≤ 3 h):** eager-A references + SDR at the sweep points; chunk
   337	  sweep (A, C3, G, H × {22050, 88200}, 3 restarts, SDR gate in r1);
